  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop a compact serial Kitamasa/polynomial-exponentiation candidate: compute x^(H-1) modulo x^N-P1*x^(N-1)-...-PN, then dot its coefficients with A. This removes dense matrices and is O(N^2 log H), O(N) memory. Reduce modulo 1e9+7 after safe 64-bit products; retain correct behavior for every permitted input (and handle a base-index request defensively).",
        "B": "Develop a target-aware parallel candidate for the same exact polynomial method. Use the four CPU cores only for sufficiently large polynomial convolutions, with race-free independent output coefficients and a serial or properly synchronized reduction phase; keep a low-overhead serial path for small N. Do not depend on OpenMP/compiler-setting changes. Measure it independently against the serial candidate, since the 20.43 ms baseline makes thread overhead material.",
        "C": "Research a higher-risk large-N candidate using Bostan-Mori on the generating function Q(x)=1-sum(Pi*x^i), with its numerator derived from A. If beneficial, accelerate convolutions with NTT-friendly auxiliary primes and exact CRT reduction back to 1e9+7; otherwise retain naive convolution. Establish indexing and signed-coefficient invariants rigorously and provide a size-based fallback, not input-specific logic.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidates that pass full correctness validation. Benchmark baseline, A, B, and C independently on the fixed evaluation process; choose one generic implementation or a justified size-based dispatch, never assuming combined gains. Preserve stdin/stdout format and modular semantics, and reject any candidate whose startup cost regresses the observed small-workload baseline.",
        "analysis": "The existing program is correct but performs dense O(N^3 log H) matrix powers, stores roughly two 1022x1022 matrices, and may copy a large matrix on return. The recurrence structure permits O(N^2 log H) exact methods. The sole baseline is valid at 20.4338 ms over four correctness cases, so it does not establish that parallelism or advanced convolution helps; selection must be measurement-led."
      },
